#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065080} :activity/id #uuid "9da5c575-7e75-5969-b8c7-04a31ed5d4a4" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048262 :topic/id #uuid "8d613936-429d-5b08-b35e-57604e0ef957" :topic/math-academy-id 322} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065080 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065360 :tutorial/content "Any number raised to the power of zero is equal to $1$. For example,\n\n$$\n2^0 = 1 \\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad (-55)^0=1\n$$\n\nTo understand why, let's try to convince ourselves that $2^0 = 1$. First, we write down a table containing all of the powers of $2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2^{4} &= 16 \\\\\n2^{3} &= 8 \\\\\n2^{2} &= 4 \\\\\n2^{1} &= 2 \\\\\n2^{0} &= ◻\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs we go down the list, the number in blue is divided by $2$ each time. Following this pattern, we must have\n\n$$\n2^{\\color{red}{0}} = {\\color{blue}{2}}\\div 2 = 1\n$$\n\nIt doesn't matter whether the number is positive or negative, a fraction or a decimal, big or small. Raising any non-zero number to the power of zero always gives $1$." :tutorial/id #uuid "73efd6db-4f12-5b29-bb69-8db83970c328" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3087 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "f6e0ef14-5163-5632-b416-f5c5aacc04ea" :step/math-academy-id 16736 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065081}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065081 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064221 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064082 :question/id #uuid "85ca537c-6cc6-5628-b349-320efba055d8" :question/math-academy-id "e-4594"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b31be039-9622-57cd-a17e-a2b8bddcd0ec" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049714}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064511} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064566}] :knowledge-point/title "Raising a Natural Number to the Power of Zero"} :step/id #uuid "df149c93-0037-5bb6-80e4-8531583426ae" :step/math-academy-id 16737 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186